======================================================================
3. CONFORMANCE TO THE SPECIFICATION
======================================================================

Overall: the code implements every feature the spec lists in section 6, but three of
the six are only partly met in behaviour, and one design instruction that the spec
depends on (section 6.3, sorting sources chronologically before the model sees them) is
not implemented in the answer path. Details:

6.1 Conversational loop ........................ MET
    chat-cli and the Tk chat window both run sanitize, cache, route, answer, citations and
    history through ChatSession.ask. /exit, /quit, /sources, /help, EOF and empty input
    behave (TC-043, 062 to 064, 066, 115). One gap: an unknown command-line argument
    opens the chat window with no usage message (TC-125, finding F-14).

6.2 Session memory for follow-ups .............. PARTLY MET
    The last three turns are kept as a question plus a 200-character summary in a labelled
    <conversation_history> block outside <documents> (confirmed by printing the prompt,
    TC-154). Pronoun and topic follow-ups work (TC-059, 060, 102, 111). Two defects:
    "the second one" is resolved to the wrong paper (TC-026) and a follow-up that refers to
    a turn outside the window is answered about the wrong topic or refused (TC-048, TC-113). Findings F-03, F-06.

6.3 Publication-year extraction and trend ...... PARTLY MET
    Year resolution exists (151 of 153 resolved, 2 unknown, natosec_40 set from its arXiv
    stamp). Two problems. (a) At least 14 files carry a filename year that the text
    contradicts (finding F-09). (b) The spec asks that retrieved sources be sorted
    chronologically BEFORE they are handed to the model, and design 4.5 says to reorder
    `items` before _answer_messages. The code sorts only the doc-id list used for the
    Sources footer (sorted_by_year is called after the answer is built), while the trend
    prompt tells the model "the documents are given to you in chronological order". The
    statement is not true, which is why the trend answers are not in year order (TC-020,
    TC-022) (finding F-02).

6.4 Reader-facing citations .................... PARTLY MET
    display_label, sources_block and rewrite_inline_citations exist and most labels are
    readable titles. Remaining defects: the same paper is listed twice in almost every
    Sources line (F-04); some labels are still file-name or front-page text ("DBLP conf sat
    2016", "DBLP journals fmsd FismanSS21", "Hana Chockler Georg Weissenbacher (Eds.)");
    and the model now writes "[paper: title | year: y]" tags into the visible answer, which
    rewrite_inline_citations does not convert (F-08).

6.5 Structured comparison answers .............. MET
    The compare addendum produces per-paper sections and an agreement/difference part
    (TC-011 to 016, 049, 060, 083). A comparison of something with itself is handled
    (TC-018).

6.6 Direct-quotation support made visible ...... PARTLY MET
    The "Quotation support: n/m" line is printed under every answer. The check itself
    has three faults: false negatives on real quotes (words split by the PDF extraction),
    quoted paper titles counted as quotes, and no check that a quote comes from the paper
    the answer names (F-01). A fabricated quote is correctly rejected (TC-095).

7. What does not change ........................ MET
    The security layer, router, cache and BM25 retrieval were kept. Injection handling holds
    (TC-035 to 040, 096 to 102, 070). Cache and guard word hold (TC-044 to 046, 105).

10. Known limitations .......................... CONFIRMED
    All five documented limitations showed up in this run: filename-based years (F-09),
    no persistence, token-overlap cache, regex router, no page numbers. Run 2 adds new
    ones that the spec does not list: duplicate documents, proceedings volumes crowding
    retrieval, no aggregation over the collection (counts, year ranges), short
    identifiers dropped by the tokenizer, and the quote-check false negatives.


