                                                  TC-123 not run
Sec 11  repository layout and README outline ... NOT CHECKED: no repository, README, .env.example or
                                                  requirements file was supplied. The code reads
                                                  requirements_8_1_v1.txt, and now six environment variables
                                                  (three answer-model, three optional planner-model).
                                                  The design text, the README outline and TC-132 still say
                                                  three; they need to be updated.

Architecture deviation (decision 8.2/9): the architecture document calls for a cheap
planner and a stronger answer model. The submission does the reverse on purpose and
documents it in the code comments ("cheaper answer model", "costlier PLANNER model", the
configuration dialog). The prices printed by the benchmark confirm it: the planner
openai/gpt-5.4-nano costs 0.20 / 1.25 USD per million tokens (in/out), the answer model
openai/gpt-4o-mini 0.15 / 0.60. Either update the architecture and spec text, or switch
the roles back. The module header (line 11) still says "a small planner model plus a
larger answer model", which contradicts the rest of the file (F-15).


======================================================================
5. FINDINGS (ordered by impact on a reviewer or a demo)
======================================================================

Severity: Blocking = wrong or invented content shown as fact, or a spec feature that does
not work; Major = visibly poor behaviour with a clear cause; Minor = cosmetic or document
drift.

F-01  Quote verification gives false results  [Blocking]  TC-027, 029, 037, 077, 092
      _quote_support normalises the quote and looks for it in the normalised corpus. The PDF
      text has line-break splits ("imple mentation", "iden tify"), so genuine quotes are
      reported as unverified (0/1 for a verbatim sketching sentence). Titles inside quotation
      marks count as quotes. And nothing checks that a verified quote comes from the paper the
      answer names: in TC-029 the "definition" attributed to the 2015 CAV paper is in the 2017
      FMSD paper, not in the 2015 one.
      Fix: compare with spaces removed on both sides; repair splits in the loader; skip spans that
      equal a known title; after verifying, list the documents that contain the quote and compare
      with the documents the answer names.

F-02  Trend answers are not in year order  [Blocking]  TC-020, 022
      See section 3, 6.3. Fix: for intent == "trend" sort the retrieved items by YEAR_BY_DOC before
      _format_docs_xml, and state "strictly ascending by year, one bullet per period" in the addendum.

F-03  Follow-ups that point into the previous answer are resolved by guesswork  [Blocking]  TC-026, 068
      History keeps 200 characters of the previous answer, so "the second one" and "the first one"
      cannot be resolved. In TC-068 "the first one" returned the wrong paper and year.
      Fix: store the ordered list of papers the previous answer presented and resolve ordinals
      against it (details in TC-026 column K).

F-04  Duplicate documents are indexed and listed twice  [Blocking for the demo]  TC-054, 058, 068, 093, 094
      The corpus has 15 groups of exact duplicates (30 files) and about 25 groups whose first 150
      characters match. Each copy is its own paper: Sources lists the same title twice in most
      answers and a duplicate pair can use two of the six retrieval slots.
      Fix: content hash at load time, canonical document plus aliases; dedupe sources_block by
      canonical id and normalised title.

F-05  Trend and "what changed" answers invent a story  [Blocking]  TC-022, 085, 089, 017
      The model builds a timeline from loosely related papers (a CAD dataset and a CAV front page
      become "sketch recognition research"), invents a "2013 journal paper" and quotes it, and
      describes a "2008 PLDI paper" that was never retrieved. The attribution note is appended
      after the invented text, and its regex only knows a short venue list (it has no "journal"),
      so it missed "2013 journal paper".
      Fix: relevance gate for trend retrieval, tell the model the year/venue claims that were not
      matched BEFORE generation, say plainly when coverage is thin (columns K of TC-022, 017, 085, 089).

F-06  Contextual retrieval pollutes the query  [Major]  TC-048, 113
      The follow-up retrieval query is the previous question plus its answer summary plus source
      titles. A new, explicit question ("remind me what CRUXEval measures") is then dominated by the
      previous topic (CEGIS oracle) and the answer is a refusal; when the referenced turn is outside
      the window the model answers about the most recent topic without saying that older turns are
      no longer kept.
      Fix: add context terms only when the new question has no topic term of its own; say "I only
      keep the last 3 turns" when an ordinal or "first question" cannot be resolved.

F-07  Proceedings volumes crowd out the right papers  [Major]  TC-057, 073, 007
      DBLP_conf_cav_BraggFRS21 (2.5 million characters, a CAV 2021 volume) appears in 29 of the
      129 Sources lines in this run, and proceedings volumes together in 42. For the BENCH CEGIS/SAT
      question it takes 3 of the 6 retrieved chunks, so neither expected sketching paper is
      retrieved (source hit 0.50 in run 1, 0.00 in run 2). Do not just delete the volumes: the
      decisive "oracle" passage for TC-007 comes from one.
      Fix: one chunk per question for volumes, rank below single-paper chunks, label them as
      "CAV 2021 proceedings volume".

F-08  The model writes titles and "[paper: ... | year: ...]" tags itself  [Major]  TC-156, many Pass rows
      The new passage prefix and the grounding addendum ("use that year and title as the paper's
      identity") make the model copy the tag into the visible answer. This breaks design decision
      5 (citations resolved after the answer, by program) and shows raw markup in a demo.
      Fix: put title and year in attributes of the passage tag, tell the model to cite by id only,
      and extend rewrite_inline_citations to convert or remove any "[paper: ...]" text.

F-09  Filename years and titles are wrong for 14 more files  [Major]  TC-020, 024, 025, 088, 091, 097
      The first page of these files is a 2025 or 2026 paper unrelated to the DBLP name: MANTRA
      (icse_JeonQFFS16, shown as 2016), Raven (pldi_SinghGS13, 2013), RVISmith (cacm_WangZKS16 2016
      and tocs_WangZKS15 2015, one paper), the knowledge-graph paper (fmcad_AlurBJMRSSSTU13 2013 and
      natosec_AlurBDF0JKMMRSSSSTU15 2015, one paper), Stencil-Lifting (pldi_KamilCIS16, 2016),
      Bench-Push (icra_CamposISK19, 2019), pldi_ChaudhuriS10 (2010), oopsla_YessenovXS11 (2011),
      popl_YangYS12 (2012), cacm_AlurSFS18 (2018), ftpl_ChaudhuriEPSSY21 (2021), ftpl_CheungS16 (2016).
      The date test: the arXiv identifier or a "to appear at ... YEAR" line on the first page is two
      or more years later than the filename year. It nominates 15 files, not 14: it also flags
      journals_corr_abs-2302-01976 (Sparling, 2023), a correct paper whose later revision says
      "to appear at ICLR 2026". The 14 listed above were each confirmed by reading the first page, so
      a date test alone is not enough; compare the first-page title as well. The test strategy lists
      3 mislabeled files and conv-check 4, so the corpus facts, the year oracle and the sanitised
      answers are all built on wrong years.
      Fix: nominate candidates with the date test, confirm by title, log the files as
      filename/content mismatches, and recompute the Corpus Facts sheet (done in
      research_assistant_test_case_v3.xlsx: 59 documents after 2020, 11 in 2016, 15 in 2025 by content).

F-10  Short identifiers are dropped by the tokenizer  [Major]  TC-078
      _tokens keeps [a-z0-9]+ and drops tokens of two characters or fewer, so "sk_p" becomes
      "sk" and "p" and is discarded; the question is reduced to "system". Fix in TC-078 column K.

F-11  The answer prompt makes the model refuse when the user's word differs  [Major]  TC-007
      Retrieval is now correct (the oracle passage ranks first). The rule "a passage that merely
      contains a word is not evidence" is read by the model as "the user's exact word must appear in
      a sentence about the topic". Fix in TC-007 column K.

F-12  Collection-wide questions get a wrong answer  [Major]  TC-025, 068 (turn 7), 091, 087, 088
      "Which papers are before 2010", "how many in 2016" cannot be answered by top-k passages. The
      assistant states "no papers before 2010" (false) or "one paper in 2016". Fix: a deterministic
      catalog route over YEAR_BY_DOC and the titles (TC-025 column K).

F-13  False "retrieved passage contained instructions" note  [Minor]  TC-046, 113
      The model is told to add that note when a document contains instructions. The corpus scan finds
      0 of 24,343 chunks matching the injection pattern, so the note is always a false alarm. The
      blocked-input wording ("after unsafe content was removed") is also wrong for harmless input such
      as "/foo" or the word "synthesis" (TC-067, 082).

F-14  Unknown command-line argument opens the chat window  [Minor]  TC-125
      Fix in TC-125 column K.

F-15  Stale text in the submission  [Minor]
      Header line 11 (small planner, larger answer model) contradicts the file; the block
      "TODO - remaining work for the 8.1 submission" is still in the file although its items are
      implemented; the benchmark constants BASELINE_EXPECTED_CALLS (16 to 22) and BASELINE_MAX_TOKENS
      (600,000) no longer describe the 153-paper BEFORE pass (954,013 tokens, 32 calls; run 1 had
      1,022,400 and 31), so the drift flag prints in every run.

F-16  Benchmark subprocess crashed once (exit code 3221225477)  [Major, cause unknown]  TC-050
      A Windows access violation in native code, no output captured, and the same command succeeded
      later in the same session (TC-073). See TC-050 column K for how to get the trace.

F-17  Refusals list unrelated sources  [Minor]
      Under "the documents do not cover this" the Sources line shows 4 to 13 unrelated papers (TC-009,
      053, 076, 097, 098, 110), which reads as support for the refusal. Show Sources only when the
      answer uses at least one passage, or label them "retrieved, not used".

Positive controls worth keeping for the recording: TC-009, 076, 084 (honest "not in the
collection"), TC-051 to 053 (mislabeled files not turned into synthesis claims), TC-034 and
TC-109 (mislabeled files answered from their real content), TC-036 to 040 and TC-070
(injection), TC-044 to 046 (cache and guard word), TC-056 and TC-072 (corpus integrity).


======================================================================
6. REVIEW OF THE TEST HARNESS (Test_Research_Assistant.py)
======================================================================

Improvements since run 1: the workbook headers are asserted, secrets are redacted, each
case runs in its own fresh chat window, manual rows are skipped with a clear message, TC-056
and TC-072 have scenarios, and heavy rows are marked.

H-01  CHECKS test the whole transcript, including the user's own turns. TC-031, 046, 049, 060
      and 061 search for words (livecodebench, cruxeval, "before 2010", "euler") that appear in
      the questions that the transcript echoes, so they pass whatever the assistant answers.
      TC-061 should assert that "euler" is ABSENT from turn 2.
H-02  TC-026 check (a 2005 to 2009 year and a 2023 to 2025 year anywhere in the transcript) is
      satisfied by turn 1 alone. It returned PASS while turn 2 answered about the wrong paper.
      Restrict the check to the last assistant reply, and for TC-026 assert the title of the
      second item of turn 1.
H-03  TC-044 and TC-045 accept a second call up to 1.25 times slower than the first. Use
      "second call under 1 second or under 20 percent of the first".
H-04  run_subprocess does not use unbuffered output or faulthandler, does not write the child's
      output to a file, and does not treat a non-zero exit code as a failure. TC-050 produced only
      "[exit code 3221225477]".
H-05  The chat window does not print token counts, so TC-044, 045, 047, 104, 105 and 113 can only
      be judged by latency. Have ChatSession.ask return usage and print it in the transcript.
H-06  The harness writes "PASS"/"FAIL" in capitals to column F; the workbook list uses "Pass" and
      "Fail". COUNTIF ignores case, but the cell values should match the list.
H-07  No automatic check for the structural criteria that the workbook relies on: duplicate titles
      in a Sources line (TC-093, 094, 068), raw "[paper:" or "DBLP" text in the answer, and "Quotation
      support" n equal to m. A single function that parses the transcript for these would grade
      about 25 rows without a person.
H-08  Manual rows that can be scripted without the LLM: TC-095, 116, 117, 118, 129, 154 (all
      were run offline in this review). Add them to PLANS as "scenario" cases so they stop
      showing as Not Run.

Status: H-01 to H-08 are implemented in the run-3 Test_Research_Assistant.py, with a self-test
(Test_Research_Assistant_selftest.py, 30 cases). Re-grading the run-2 results with the new checks
gives the same Pass or Fail as the graded workbook on all 42 rows where it grades Pass or Fail
(no contradiction). On the other rows it leaves F empty and prints a lint note, or marks Not Run.


